{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "05PY", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\text{id}_X \\in \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(X, X)$", "MATH_0002": "$X \\to M_i$", "MATH_0003": "$i$", "MATH_0004": "$X \\to M_i \\to X$", "MATH_0005": "$$\n\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, X)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\colim_i \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, M_i)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, X)\n$$", "MATH_0006": "$W$", "MATH_0007": "$X \\to M_i$", "MATH_0008": "$$\n\\colim_i \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, M_i)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, X)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\colim_i \\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(W, M_i)\n$$", "MATH_0009": "$W = M_j$", "MATH_0010": "$\\text{id}_{M_j}$", "MATH_0011": "$M_j \\to X \\to M_i \\to M_k$", "MATH_0012": "$M_j \\to M_k$", "MATH_0013": "$k$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\n\n\\medskip\\noindent\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_text": "It is clear that (3) implies (2). Assume (2).\nThen <MATH_0001>\ncomes from a morphism <MATH_0002> for some <MATH_0003>, i.e., <MATH_0004>\nis the identity. Then both maps\n<MATH_0005>\nare bijective for all <MATH_0006> where the first one is induced by the morphism\n<MATH_0007> we found above, and the composition is the identity. This means\nthat the composition\n<MATH_0008>\nis the identity too. Setting <MATH_0009> and starting with <MATH_0010>\nin the colimit, we see that <MATH_0011> is equal to\n<MATH_0012> for some <MATH_0013> large enough. This proves (1) holds.", "unit_id": "tag:05PY:proof-p001", "source_text_hash": "sha256:12b69975e120139c173a17c034d0f045a9c1d22f7fbd61ed3b24a111b80fe02b", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:6b07361e760c995053a6ee176a838a74b0e5840a9125800f4394fd6f38d7d4da", "source_math_hash": "sha256:9b36ecd611f2c5ab5f679ecf27a14386f26b537cde685efa3d6c47a4b6b9183a"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "05PY", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$k$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0003": "$W = M_k$", "MATH_0004": "$M_k \\to X$", "MATH_0005": "$j$", "MATH_0006": "$k \\to j$", "MATH_0007": "$i \\to j$", "MATH_0008": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0009": "$M_k \\to X \\to M_i \\to M_j$", "MATH_0010": "$M_k \\to M_j$", "MATH_0011": "$(X, \\{M_k \\to X\\})$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\n\\medskip\\noindent\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_text": "Assume (4). Let <MATH_0001> be an object of <MATH_0002>. Setting <MATH_0003>\nwe deduce there exists a unique morphism <MATH_0004> such that\nthere exists a <MATH_0005> and morphisms <MATH_0006> and\n<MATH_0007> in <MATH_0008> such that <MATH_0009>\nis equal to <MATH_0010>. The uniqueness guarantees that we obtain a\ncocone <MATH_0011>. In this way we see that (4) implies (2);\nsome details omitted.", "unit_id": "tag:05PY:proof-p002", "source_text_hash": "sha256:8bbb37f43d67bed5eeb66e4c28ea327bbad16768ed3c4c43bd0f134a8041954a", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:d5b7ed19b5143a67e4a930e5ffa409c6a39518d79461bc80962a02256c731147", "source_math_hash": "sha256:a7992c0cde8601f16b10fe7f3c48bf35cf56f86a3347e3b295cf6ab04c7c63f7"}
